/*!
@defgroup ee_emulation
@ingroup mod_flash EEPROM Emulation
@brief Flash-backed fixed-size EEPROM emulation module for the YTMicro SDK.

@details
The EEPROM Emulation module stores fixed-size logical records inside a
round-robin pool of Flash sectors. It reconstructs the active-sector window at
startup, writes updated records by appending new copies, and retires sectors
that cannot be recovered during erase or program flows. This approach provides
non-volatile parameter storage on devices that do not include native EEPROM.

@section ee_emulation_features Key Features
- Flash-backed emulation of fixed-size EEPROM records.
- Round-robin active and ready sector pool with sector-swap recovery.
- Automatic reconstruction of active-sector state during initialization.
- Optional cache table for accelerating record lookups.
- Optional callback service while long Flash commands are pending.
- Retry-and-retire policy for failed erase, program, or sector-state updates.
- Compatible with data-Flash or main-array Flash devices through
  `FEATURE_EFM_*` feature macros.

@section ee_emulation_operations Supported Operations

| Operation | Primary APIs | Description |
|-----------|--------------|-------------|
| Pool initialization | Eed_InitEeprom() | Removes dead sectors, repairs incomplete swap state, initializes missing sector headers, and rebuilds the optional cache. |
| Record write | Eed_WriteEeprom() | Appends a new record image and performs a sector swap when the current active write sector is full. |
| Record read | Eed_ReadEeprom() | Returns the newest valid copy of one logical record. |
| Pool erase | Eed_DeinitEeprom() | Erases every sector assigned to the emulation pool. |
| Internal swap support | Eed_CopyData(), Eed_ValidateActIndicator(), Eed_SyncValidateSector() | Moves live records into the ready sector and finalizes sector-state transitions. |
| Low-level Flash access | Eed_FlashErase(), Eed_FlashEraseVerify(), Eed_FlashProgram() | Issues erase and program commands and verifies the result. |

@section ee_emulation_arch Module Architecture

The EEPROM Emulation module uses a layered architecture:

@verbatim
┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│                      Application Code                     │
│          (calls Eed_InitEeprom / Read / Write / Deinit)   │
├────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│        High-Level Service Layer (ee_emulation.h/c)        │
│  ● Pool lifecycle management                              │
│  ● Public read / write record services                    │
├────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│        Middle-Level Runtime Layer (ee_hardware.h/c)       │
│  ● Record copy and append helpers                         │
│  ● Sector recovery, cache rebuild, ordering maintenance   │
├────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│        Low-Level Flash Access Layer (ee_low_level.c)      │
│  ● EFM erase / program command launch                     │
│  ● Read-back verification                                 │
├────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│                  Flash / EFM Hardware                     │
│    (sector erase, write-unit program, status polling)     │
└────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
@endverbatim

@subsection ee_emulation_arch_public Public Driver Layer
Application code should include `ee_emulation.h` and use only the public
high-level services. The application supplies the sector map through
`eeprom_config_t::startSecAddr` and the compile-time configuration through
`ee_user_config.h`.

@subsection ee_emulation_arch_internal Internal Helper Layers
The middle-level helpers maintain sector order, cache state, and sector-swap
recovery. The low-level helpers issue EFM erase or program commands and verify
the resulting Flash contents.

@section ee_emulation_config Configuration Parameters

@subsection ee_emulation_config_state eeprom_config_t Fields
| Field | Type | Description | Filled By |
|-------|------|-------------|-----------|
| startSecAddr | `uint32_t[EE_ALLOCATED_SECTORS]` | Base address of every Flash sector assigned to EEPROM emulation. | Application |
| blankSpace | `uint32_t` | Address of the next writable record slot in the youngest active sector. | Driver |
| eraseCycValue | `uint32_t` | Erase-cycle value tracked for the current write sector. | Driver |
| maxValidIndex | `uint32_t` | Highest live sector index after dead sectors are removed from the round-robin list. | Driver |

@subsection ee_emulation_config_user User Configuration Macros
The following macros are expected from `ee_user_config.h`:

| Macro | Description | Typical Value |
|-------|-------------|---------------|
| EE_EEPROM_SIZE | Total logical EEPROM size to emulate, in bytes. | Application-specific |
| EE_DATA_VALUE_SIZE | Payload size of one logical record, in bytes. | Application-specific |
| EE_EXTRA_READY_SECTORS | Additional ready-sector budget reserved for retiring failed sectors. | `0x0U` or `0x1U` |
| EE_EXTRA_ACTIVE_SECTORS | Additional active-sector budget reserved beyond the minimum required capacity. | `0x0U` or `0x2U` |
| EE_SYS_GLOBAL_IRQ_DISABLE | Controls whether the RAM-resident Flash command launcher masks global interrupts internally. | `false` |
| EE_CALLBACK_ENABLE | Enables the optional callback service. | `false` |
| EE_CALLBACK_COUNTER | Number of polling iterations between callback invocations when callbacks are enabled. | `0x4U` |
| EE_CACHE_TABLE_MAX_ENTRY | Number of record identifiers cached in RAM for faster lookup. | `0x1U` |
| EE_REERASE_NUM | Additional erase retries before a sector is retired. | `0x0U` to `0x3U` |
| EE_REPROGRAM_NUM | Additional indicator-program retries before a sector is retired. | `0x0U` to `0x3U` |
| EE_START_ADDRESS | Base Flash address used by the application when it builds `startSecAddr`. | Application-specific |

@subsection ee_emulation_config_features Device Feature Macros
| Macro | Description |
|-------|-------------|
| FEATURE_EFM_WRITE_UNIT_SIZE | Flash write granularity used for record headers and sector-state indicators. |
| FEATURE_EFM_HAS_DATA_FLASH | Selects whether EEPROM emulation uses dedicated data Flash or the main Flash array. |
| FEATURE_EFM_DATA_ARRAY_SECTOR_SIZE | Physical data-Flash sector size when data Flash is present. |
| FEATURE_EFM_MAIN_ARRAY_SECTOR_SIZE | Physical main-array sector size when main Flash is used for emulation. |
| FEATURE_EFM_ERASE_SECTOR_CMD_CODE | EFM command code used to erase one sector. |
| FEATURE_EFM_PROGRAM_CMD_CODE | EFM command code used to program one write unit. |
| FEATURE_EFM_CMD_STS_CLEAR_MASK | Status-mask value used to clear stale EFM command flags. |
| FEATURE_EFM_CMD_ERROR_MASK | Status-mask value used to detect EFM command errors. |

@section ee_emulation_usage Usage Patterns

@subsection ee_emulation_usage_init Pattern 1: Build the Sector Map and Initialize the Pool
This pattern prepares the runtime state, maps every allocated Flash sector, and
reconstructs the EEPROM emulation pool.

@code{.c}
#include "ee_emulation.h"

status_t EepromPool_Init(void)
{
    status_t status;
    uint32_t i;
    eeprom_config_t eepromConfig;

    for (i = 0U; i < EE_ALLOCATED_SECTORS; i++)
    {
        eepromConfig.startSecAddr[i] = EE_START_ADDRESS + (i * EE_SECTOR_SIZE);
    }

    status = Eed_InitEeprom(&eepromConfig);
    return status;
}
@endcode

@subsection ee_emulation_usage_rw Pattern 2: Write and Read One Record
This pattern initializes the pool, writes one record, and reads the same record
back for verification.

@code{.c}
#include "ee_emulation.h"

status_t EepromRecord_WriteAndRead(void)
{
    status_t status;
    uint32_t i;
    uint8_t writeData[EE_DATA_VALUE_SIZE];
    uint8_t readData[EE_DATA_VALUE_SIZE];
    eeprom_config_t eepromConfig;

    for (i = 0U; i < EE_ALLOCATED_SECTORS; i++)
    {
        eepromConfig.startSecAddr[i] = EE_START_ADDRESS + (i * EE_SECTOR_SIZE);
    }

    for (i = 0U; i < EE_DATA_VALUE_SIZE; i++)
    {
        writeData[i] = (uint8_t)i;
    }

    status = Eed_InitEeprom(&eepromConfig);
    status |= Eed_WriteEeprom(&eepromConfig, 0U, writeData);
    status |= Eed_ReadEeprom(&eepromConfig, 0U, readData);

    if (status == STATUS_SUCCESS)
    {
        for (i = 0U; i < EE_DATA_VALUE_SIZE; i++)
        {
            if (writeData[i] != readData[i])
            {
                status = STATUS_ERROR;
                break;
            }
        }
    }

    return status;
}
@endcode

@subsection ee_emulation_usage_reinit Pattern 3: Erase the Pool Before a Fresh Start
This pattern is useful during bring-up when the selected Flash region contains
old test data and the application wants to force a clean initialization.

@code{.c}
#include "ee_emulation.h"

status_t EepromPool_Reinitialize(void)
{
    status_t status;
    uint32_t i;
    eeprom_config_t eepromConfig;

    for (i = 0U; i < EE_ALLOCATED_SECTORS; i++)
    {
        eepromConfig.startSecAddr[i] = EE_START_ADDRESS + (i * EE_SECTOR_SIZE);
    }

    status = Eed_DeinitEeprom(&eepromConfig);
    if (status == STATUS_SUCCESS)
    {
        status = Eed_InitEeprom(&eepromConfig);
    }

    return status;
}
@endcode

@section ee_emulation_api API Quick Reference

@subsection ee_emulation_api_public Public Driver Layer
| Function | Description |
|----------|-------------|
| Eed_InitEeprom() | Discover live sectors, repair incomplete sector state, and rebuild the runtime cache. |
| Eed_WriteEeprom() | Append a new logical record and perform a sector swap when required. |
| Eed_ReadEeprom() | Read the newest valid copy of one logical record. |
| Eed_DeinitEeprom() | Erase every sector assigned to the EEPROM emulation pool. |

@subsection ee_emulation_api_runtime Internal Runtime Helpers
| Function | Description |
|----------|-------------|
| Eed_CopyData() | Copy the newest valid records from the oldest active sector into the ready swap sector. |
| Eed_WriteRecord() | Program one record payload and header into the current blank slot. |
| Eed_SearchBlankSpace() | Locate the first blank record slot in one active sector. |
| Eed_SearchInAllActives() | Search all active sectors from newest to oldest for one record ID. |
| Eed_SearchLoop() | Search the optional cache first and then fall back to an active-sector scan. |
| Eed_CallBack() | Invoke the registered callback after the configured polling interval. |
| Eed_ValidateDeadIndicator() | Mark one sector as dead and compact the live sector list. |
| Eed_SyncValidateSector() | Erase and reinitialize one sector, retiring it on repeated failure. |
| Eed_ValidateActIndicator() | Program the active indicator for one sector. |
| Eed_OverProgram() | Retry one sector-state write until it reads back or the retry limit is exhausted. |
| Eed_InitAllSectors() | Reconstruct active and ready sector state during initialization. |
| Eed_ShiftIdxToBase() | Rotate the sector-address array so a selected sector becomes the base active position. |
| Eed_RemoveDeadSector() | Compact dead sectors out of the live round-robin window. |
| Eed_UpdateCacheTable() | Clear or rebuild cached record locations. |

@subsection ee_emulation_api_flash Internal Flash Access Helpers
| Function | Description |
|----------|-------------|
| Eed_FlashErase() | Erase one Flash sector and verify that it is blank. |
| Eed_FlashEraseVerify() | Verify that a Flash region contains only erased long words. |
| Eed_FlashProgram() | Program one Flash region and verify the written data. |

@section ee_emulation_notes Important Notes

@subsection ee_emulation_notes_flash Dedicated Flash Region Required
The sector list supplied through `startSecAddr` must reference a Flash region
reserved exclusively for EEPROM emulation. Reusing those sectors for code,
calibration tables, or other data will corrupt the emulated EEPROM contents.

@subsection ee_emulation_notes_record Fixed Record Size
Every record uses the compile-time payload length `EE_DATA_VALUE_SIZE`.
Applications must provide exactly that many readable bytes for writes and
writable bytes for reads.

@subsection ee_emulation_notes_recovery Swap Recovery Behavior
The module appends new records and then swaps sectors when the youngest active
sector becomes full. Initialization reconstructs interrupted swap state and can
retire sectors that fail erase, program, or indicator validation.

@warning If the module runs out of ready sectors because too many sectors were
retired, initialization or write flows can fail with
`STATUS_EdPROM_MAKE_DEAD_ERROR`.

@subsection ee_emulation_notes_callback Callback Execution Requirements
When `EE_CALLBACK_ENABLE` is enabled, the callback is invoked from inside the
Flash-command polling flow. The callback implementation must be safe to execute
while Flash is busy, and platforms that require RAM execution for such code
must place the callback in RAM.

@subsection ee_emulation_notes_interrupt Interrupt Masking
The low-level Flash launcher can mask global interrupts when
`EE_SYS_GLOBAL_IRQ_DISABLE` is enabled. Choose this setting according to the
target device Flash-access restrictions and the application's interrupt
latency budget.

@section ee_emulation_integration Integration Guide

@subsection ee_emulation_integration_files Compilation Units
The following source files must be added to the project build:
@verbatim
${SDK_PATH}/platform/drivers/src/ee_emulation/ee_high_level.c
${SDK_PATH}/platform/drivers/src/ee_emulation/ee_middle_level.c
${SDK_PATH}/platform/drivers/src/ee_emulation/ee_low_level.c
@endverbatim

@subsection ee_emulation_integration_include Include Paths
Add the following directories to the toolchain include path:
@verbatim
${SDK_PATH}/platform/drivers/inc/
${SDK_PATH}/platform/drivers/src/ee_emulation/
@endverbatim

The project-specific directory that contains `ee_user_config.h` must also be
added to the include path.

@subsection ee_emulation_integration_symbols Compile Symbols
No extra compiler `-D` symbols are required when the project already provides
`ee_user_config.h`. All module-specific configuration is expected to come from
that header.

@subsection ee_emulation_integration_deps Dependencies
- `ee_user_config.h` - Application-provided compile-time configuration.
- `device_registers.h` - Device register definitions and `FEATURE_EFM_*`
  capability macros.
- `interrupt_manager.h` - Global interrupt control used during Flash erase and
  program sequences.

*/
